  "answer_core": "Do not pick a dissertation direction yet. Treat the next two weeks as a short, low‑cost probe with two concrete gates.\n\nGate 1 (Silva/data): Confirm whether the Silva direction can actually become a dissertation. Ask what data already exists, who controls access, when you can see it, what collaboration or authorship terms would be, and what technical gaps would require support from others. Make these checks with your advisor’s knowledge. Use Dr. Silva only to verify data availability, collaboration depth, and timelines—not to make a political commitment.\n\nGate 2 (fallback viability): Confirm that your fallback direction is genuinely alive. That means your advisor explicitly supports keeping it resourced, that you still have access to the needed materials and collaborators, and that you have a clear stop‑date and criteria for returning to it if the Silva probe fails.\n\nSequence: speak with your advisor first, run the short Silva probe second, and only involve the committee after you know whether the data path is real and whether your advisor would back a shift without undermining your fallback.\n\nChoose your direction only after both gates have concrete evidence. Focus on what can actually turn into a finishable dissertation, not on which story feels more exciting.",
